Dependency Injection (DI) Using Annotations :
Code Example :

StudentService.java--> 

package com.example.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class StudentService 
{
    public void display()  
    {
        System.out.println("Student Service Called");
    }
}
//@Service --> Marks class as a Service Bean.

StudentController.java -->

package com.example.controller;

import com.example.service.StudentService;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;

@Controller
public class StudentController 
{
    @Autowired
    private StudentService service;

    public void execute() 
    {
        service.display();
    }
}
//@Controller --> Marks class as Controller Bean.
//@Autowired --> Inject StudentService automatically.
//public void execute() --> Calls service.

Main Class-->

package com.example;

import com.example.controller.StudentController;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.AnnotationConfigApplicationContext;

public class App 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        ApplicationContext context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext("com.example");
        StudentController controller = context.getBean(StudentController.class);
        controller.execute();
    }
}
//IOC Container --> ApplicationContext context, It Stores beans.
//new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext("com.example"); --> Searches for annotations.
//controller.execute(); --> Runs controller method.

Types of Dependency Injection :
1. Field Injection : Spring injects directly into field.
2. Setter Injection
3. Constructor Injection (Most preferred in modern Spring)
